Appendix A: Formal Mathematical Definition of the Research Node

To avoid the ambiguities of software-specific schemas (e.g., YAML), the research node is defined here as a formal mathematical tuple. This structure is the foundational unit of G_{know} and G_{exec}.

Definition A.1 (The Augmented Research Node).
A research node v_i is defined as an 8-tuple:

v_i = \langle I_i, S_i, O_i, T_i, A_i, \mathcal{V}_i, \mathcal{U}_i, \mathcal{P}_i \rangle 

Where:

1. I_i \subseteq \bigcup_{j} O_j: The set of input artifacts (identified by content hashes).
2. S_i: The scope constraints and permissions.
3. O_i: The expected output artifact.
4. T_i \in \{T_D, T_J, T_E, T_H\}: The task type (Deterministic, Judgment, Empirical, Hybrid).
5. A_i \in \{1, 0, \bot\}: The acceptance state (Accepted, Rejected, Unresolved).
6. \mathcal{V}_i = \langle Q^{struct}_i, Q^{det}_i, Q^{logical}_i, Q^{adv}_i \rangle: The tuple of typed verification gates.
7. \mathcal{U}_i = \langle U^{qual}_i, U^{int}_i, U^{prob}_i, U^{conf}_i \rangle: The four-tier uncertainty record.
8. \mathcal{P}_i = \langle \text{prov:Entity}, \text{prov:Activity}, \text{prov:Agent} \rangle: The W3C PROV mapping for lineage traceability.

---

Appendix B: Formal State-Transition System for Truth Maintenance

The Justification-based Truth Maintenance System (JTMS) described in Section 6 is formalized here as a state-transition system. This defines the dependency-directed invalidation cascade.

Definition B.1 (Justification System).
Let \mathcal{J}: V \rightarrow \mathcal{P}(V) be a function mapping each node to its justification set (the set of predecessor nodes it directly depends on).

Definition B.2 (Invalidation Operator).
Let \mathcal{I}: V \rightarrow V be the invalidation operator defined recursively. For a failed node v_k (where A_k = 0), the set of invalidated nodes is:

\mathcal{I}^*(v_k) = \{ v_m \in V \mid v_k \in \mathcal{J}(v_m) \} \cup \bigcup_{v_m \in \mathcal{I}^*(v_k)} \mathcal{I}^*(v_m) 

Theorem B.1 (Zombie Fact Prevention).
If a node v_k is rejected (A_k = 0), then for all v_m \in \mathcal{I}^*(v_k), the acceptance state transitions to A_m = \bot.
Proof: By Definition B.2, any v_m in the transitive closure of \mathcal{J} rooted at v_k relies on v_k. If v_k is invalid, the justification for v_m is broken. Thus, v_m cannot retain the state A_m = 1 and must transition to unresolved (\bot).

---

Appendix C: Theoretical Formulation of Conformal Prediction Sets

To ground the U^{conf} uncertainty tier without empirical calibration data, we define the theoretical guarantee required by the protocol.

Let \mathcal{X} be the input space (e.g., prompts) and \mathcal{Y} be the output space (e.g., mathematical claims). Let P be an unknown distribution over \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}.

Definition C.1 (Conformal Predictor).
A conformal predictor is a set-valued function C_\alpha: \mathcal{X} \rightarrow \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y}) that satisfies the marginal coverage guarantee:

\mathbb{P}_{(X, Y) \sim P} (Y \in C_\alpha(X)) \ge 1 - \alpha 

Where \alpha \in (0, 1) is the user-specified error rate.

Protocol Requirement: The MRGP requires that any node output O_i containing an U^{conf} tier must document the coverage \alpha and the size of the theoretical calibration set required to achieve it. If an agent cannot provide a statistically valid conformal prediction set, U^{conf} defaults to \emptyset, and the downstream Q^{logical} gate must apply a maximal strictness penalty.

