Appendix D: Formal Experimental Design for the Seeded-Error Harness

Since no experiments have been run, this appendix defines the statistical framework for the Tier 1 mechanism validation (Section 7). It specifies how to calculate the Verification-Cost Error (VCE) Pareto frontier and the False-Containment Rate (FCR) in future empirical trials.

D.1 Expected Values over Seeded Distributions

Let \mathcal{D}_{seeded} be a probability distribution over adversarial errors (e.g., mutated numerical values, dropped caveats, hallucinated citations). Let \mathcal{D}_{correct} be the distribution of correct, verified artifacts.

Definition D.1 (VCE and FCR Functions).
For a verification budget B:

VCE(B) = \mathbb{E}_{e \sim \mathcal{D}_{seeded}} [ \mathbb{I}(\text{Gate fails to catch } e \mid B) ] 

FCR(B) = \mathbb{E}_{c \sim \mathcal{D}_{correct}} [ \mathbb{I}(\text{Gate rejects } c \mid B) ] 

D.2 The Pareto Frontier

The optimal operating point for a given deployment is not a single scalar but a set of non-dominated solutions.

Definition D.2 (VCE-FCR Pareto Frontier).
Let \mathcal{B} be the set of all valid budget allocations. The Pareto frontier \mathcal{F} is the set of tuples (B, VCE(B), FCR(B)) such that no other budget B' exists where VCE(B') \le VCE(B) and FCR(B') \le FCR(B), with at least one strict inequality.

Theoretical Expectation: As budget B increases, the protocol theorizes that VCE(B) monotonically decreases (fewer missed errors) while FCR(B) monotonically increases (more Type I errors due to over-verification). This trade-off defines the fundamental limit of the protocol's empirical validation.


